+<h1>Huawei / UniVPN protocol observations</h1>
+
+<p>These are initial observations for the Huawei / UniVPN SSL VPN
+investigation in <a
+href="https://gitlab.com/openconnect/openconnect/-/issues/603">issue #603</a>.
+They describe a subset exercised by a separate TCP forwarding prototype
+against one authorized gateway. They do not establish compatibility with
+all Huawei or Leagsoft firmware, and do not add a working protocol to
+OpenConnect.</p>
+
+<p>Only password authentication and IPv4 over two TLS connections were
+exercised. Unknown fields below retain their observed values; their
+semantics have not been established. Integers are in network byte order.</p>
+
+<h2>Framing</h2>
+
+<p>Following the initial request, both TLS channels use an eight-byte
+header followed by the indicated payload:</p>
+
+<table>
+<tr><th>Offset</th><th>Length</th><th>Observed field</th></tr>
+<tr><td>0</td><td>4</td><td>Magic <tt>c1 92 a4 d6</tt></td></tr>
+<tr><td>4</td><td>1</td><td>Version <tt>01</tt></td></tr>
+<tr><td>5</td><td>1</td><td>Command</td></tr>
+<tr><td>6</td><td>2</td><td>Payload length, excluding header</td></tr>
+<tr><td>8</td><td>variable</td><td>Payload</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<p>TLVs consist of a two-byte type, a two-byte length and that many value
+bytes. An interrupted or partial TLS read must retain the current frame,
+including when a timeout occurs immediately after its header. A new TLS
+record does not necessarily begin a new protocol frame.</p>
+
+<h2>Control connection</h2>
+
+<p>The initial request is 340 bytes:</p>
+
+<pre>
+fe fc ef be c1 92 a4 d6 00 00 00 00 00 1d 01 44
+[64 zero bytes]
+[authentication domain, UTF-8, zero-padded to 256 bytes]
+00 01 00 00
+</pre>
+
+<p>The prototype requires a framed command <tt>00</tt> reply whose payload
+is four zero bytes. It then sends command <tt>01</tt> with a 256-byte padded
+domain, four zero bytes and the following TLVs:</p>
+
+<table>
+<tr><th>Type</th><th>Value</th></tr>
+<tr><td>1</td><td>Username, escaped and NUL-terminated</td></tr>
+<tr><td>2</td><td>Password, escaped and NUL-terminated</td></tr>
+<tr><td>3</td><td>Client MAC string, such as <tt>0200-0000-0001</tt></td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<p>The observed escaping replaces <tt>%</tt>, <tt>&amp;</tt> and <tt>+</tt>
+with <tt>%25</tt>, <tt>%26</tt> and <tt>%2B</tt>, respectively. The
+prototype uses a deterministic locally administered MAC value; requirements
+for that field on other gateways are unknown.</p>
+
+<p>A successful command <tt>01</tt> response begins with a four-byte zero
+result. TLVs begin at byte 8 of the response payload; the first 32 bytes of
+type 3 supply a session token. The intervening four bytes are not interpreted.
+Passwords and session tokens must not be included in diagnostic reports.</p>
+
+<p>Command <tt>03</tt> requests a virtual address, with the 32-byte token as
+payload. The tested successful response begins with a zero byte. Starting
+at payload byte 4, a type 1 TLV of length 5 contains the assigned IPv4 address
+in its first four bytes. The fifth byte is not interpreted.</p>
+
+<h2>Data connection and heartbeats</h2>
+
+<p>A second TLS connection sends command <tt>64</tt> with the session token.
+The tested response has command <tt>64</tt> and one zero payload byte.</p>
+
+<table>
+<tr><th>Channel</th><th>Command</th><th>Payload</th><th>Prototype interval</th></tr>
+<tr><td>Control</td><td><tt>06</tt></td><td>32-byte token followed by a
+four-byte elapsed-millisecond counter</td><td>10 seconds</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Data</td><td><tt>65</tt></td><td>Empty</td><td>15 seconds</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Data</td><td><tt>66</tt></td><td>Eight zero bytes followed by an
+IPv4 packet</td><td>As needed</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<p>The millisecond counter is measured since session setup and truncated
+to 32 bits. The prototype reads control replies while data is idle, and
+serializes heartbeat and packet writes on each TLS channel. Reply semantics
+and whether the server requires these exact intervals need further study.</p>
+
+<h2>Interoperability evidence and remaining work</h2>
+
+<p>On macOS, a fresh SSH connection through the prototype, binary transfers
+of 256 KiB in each direction, a 65-second idle SSH connection and a concurrent
+second connection passed. SSH host-key verification remained enabled and
+existing SSH multiplexed connections were not reused. A wrong TLS certificate
+pin was rejected before VPN authentication.</p>
+
+<p>The gateway certificate did not chain to the system CA store. The live
+test used a first-observed full SHA-256 certificate pin; no independent
+verification of that initial certificate identity is claimed. A production
+implementation must use normal certificate validation or a pin obtained
+through a trusted channel.</p>
+
+<p>The prototype constructs a minimal IPv4/TCP flow, not a complete TCP
+stack. An OpenConnect implementation should instead pass complete IPv4
+packets to and from TUN and let the operating system handle TCP. It should
+reuse OpenConnect's certificate validation, authentication and event-loop
+interfaces.</p>
+
+<p>Other authentication methods, firmware versions, IPv6, UDP/DTLS,
+compression, session renewal, disconnect messages, flow control and
+multi-hour stability remain unverified. Further redacted observations and
+an implementation following the existing
+<a href="mitm.html">protocol investigation guidance</a> are welcome.</p>
+
